  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged stretch of dry weather through the first \nhalf of the upcoming weekend. Seasonably cool temperatures Tuesday \nthrough midweek followed by period of warmer temperatures later in \nthe work week. \n\nA cold front pushes southward into southern New England tonight. \nThis front will be fairly moisture-starved limiting it's impacts to \na period of breezy northerly winds overnight accompanied by \nadvection of a cooler airmass. \n\nUpper ridging and surface high pressure will dominate through the \nupcoming weekend favoring dry weather. Southern New England will be \nin a cooler post-frontal airmass Tuesday with 925mb temperatures \nranging +1C to +4C. This will support high temperatures in the mid \nto upper 50s. Locations along the Cape/Islands and even portions of \nthe east coast may be limited to the low 50s given the N/NNE onshore \nwind component. The cool airmass in place combined with light winds \nand clear skies will favor efficient radiational cooling Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday. Temperatures are likely to fall into the upper \n20s and 30s. The higher terrain, valleys, and other radiator spots \nhave potential to see mid 20s with even a some spots in the low 20s! \n\nSimilar conditions prevail Wednesday. Temperatures trend slightly \nwarmer as a mid level ridge shifts further east into the region \naccompanied by warmer temperatures aloft. The warming trend in \ntemperatures continues into late-week resulting in 925mb \ntemperatures warming to +10 to +13C. This will yield high \ntemperatures in the mid-60s on Thursday which is seasonably warm for \nthis time of year. Despite the mid-level pattern becoming more zonal \nFriday (ahead of our next weak wave), the westerly flow will support \nefficient mixing in the boundary. This will result in a breezy, but \nlikely the warmest day of the week with high temperatures in the \nupper 60s and even low 70s in portions of southern New England. NBM \nhighlights moderate to high probabilities(60%-80%+) for 70F or \ngreater across the valleys, CT, and I-95 corridor. Therefore a \nlarger portion of southern New England, with the exception of the \nhigher elevations and Cape/Islands, will have a good shot at hitting \nthe 70 degree mark on Friday. \n\nAnother moisture-starved shortwave and cold front moves overnight \nFriday. This will bring back temperatures into the low 60s for the \nweekend which is seasonable for late-October. Looking further ahead \nto the latter half of the weekend and next week, ensemble guidance \nshows agreement with a transition to a generally unsettled pattern \nwhich may feature periodic chances for showers. Confidence decreases \nwith respect to the details such as timing.",
